---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';

const path = '/compare/klaviyo-vs-yotpo-email-sms';
const title = "Klaviyo vs Yotpo Email & SMS: Migration and Module Continuity";
const desc = "Klaviyo after Yotpo Email/SMS retirement: Shopify profile and event migration, Reviews and Loyalty integration, queue ownership and matched costs.";

const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is this still a choice between two active messaging products?",
    "answer": "No. Yotpo Email/SMS is deprecated and SMS is decommissioned. Evaluate Klaviyo as an active replacement while deciding separately what to do with continuing Reviews and Loyalty."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should I remove Yotpo Reviews when moving email?",
    "answer": "Not automatically. Preserve useful review content and product mappings if keeping Reviews meets requirements. Confirm the Klaviyo integration scope and choose one owner for review requests."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should I quote 20,000 contacts?",
    "answer": "Separate billable profiles, eligible sends and SMS usage. Include continuing modules, integration, migration and labor over the same calendar; historical Yotpo messaging pricing is not a current offer."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can Klaviyo and Yotpo coexist?",
    "answer": "Klaviyo can provide messaging alongside continuing Reviews and Loyalty through supported integration scope. Define identifiers, freshness, permissions and queue ownership rather than assuming every old workflow transfers."
  },
  {
    "question": "Will predictive CLV work immediately?",
    "answer": "Do not assume it. Klaviyo has data requirements, and an individual profile can lack predictions. Test field availability and use a deterministic fallback where needed."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should browse abandonment be accepted?",
    "answer": "Verify tracking for an identified test profile, product mapping and purchase exits. Test anonymous and late-order cases separately; neither a Shopify tag nor imported profile proves Viewed Product tracking."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should Sequenzy be added during migration?",
    "answer": "Only evaluate another sender against a documented unmet job and matched costs. Adding a sender creates another permission and queue boundary; no automatic winner is established here."
  },
  {
    "question": "What test settles readiness?",
    "answer": "Reconcile identities and suppression, then test welcome, cart, review and loyalty jobs with late orders, returns and replayed events. Require traces and assigned incident owners before approving each flow."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm">
        <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-[#5d3a1d]">/</span>
        <span class="text-[#5d3a1d]">Klaviyo vs Yotpo Email & SMS</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="catalog-kicker">Head-to-head</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Klaviyo vs Yotpo Email & SMS for Shopify email tools</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">Yotpo messaging has retired. The current decision is whether Klaviyo can take the required lifecycle jobs while useful Reviews and Loyalty data remain connected, with explicit identity, event and sender ownership.</p>
      </header>

      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox picks={[{"label": "Active lifecycle candidate", "tool": "Klaviyo", "reason": "Validate Shopify events and required email/SMS jobs."}, {"label": "Retired messaging", "tool": "Yotpo Email & SMS", "reason": "Not a current purchase or renewal alternative."}, {"label": "Content continuity", "tool": "Reviews / Loyalty", "reason": "Confirm module-specific data and request ownership."}, {"label": "Predictive branches", "tool": "Data eligibility", "reason": "Use a deterministic fallback when fields are absent."}]} />
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto">
        <h2 class="px-5 py-4 font-serif text-2xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Comparison at a glance</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Klaviyo</th><th>Yotpo Email & SMS</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody><tr><td>Status</td><td>Active email/SMS automation</td><td>Email/SMS retired</td></tr><tr><td>Browse</td><td>Configure and verify identified tracking</td><td>Rebuild required flows in an active sender</td></tr><tr><td>Reviews / Loyalty</td><td>Supported integration scope must pass</td><td>Continuing products, separate from messaging</td></tr><tr><td>Historical data</td><td>Map identities, fields and event gaps</td><td>Use available saved records; no dashboard promise</td></tr><tr><td>Procurement</td><td>Matched active quote and workflow acceptance</td><td>Retained-module costs only, not messaging renewal</td></tr></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
<h2>Messaging retirement changes the decision</h2>
<p>As checked November 25, 2026, Yotpo’s <a class="link" href="https://email-and-sms.yotpo.com/">decommission notice</a> says the SMS dashboard and background services are inactive. Its <a class="link" href="https://www.yotpo.com/blog/email-sms-migration-partners/">migration announcement</a> scheduled full Email and SMS deprecation for December 31, 2025. SMSBump is historical naming here. Yotpo Email is not a separately available fallback. Reviews and Loyalty remain separate continuing products; keeping them does not restore messaging. These proposed exercises report no merchant deployment, measured lift or completed migration.</p><h2>What Klaviyo can contribute now</h2>
<p><a class="link" href="https://www.klaviyo.com/solutions/marketing-automation">Klaviyo</a> offers email and SMS automation and segmentation. Shopify <a class="link" href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/4425956184731">onsite tracking</a> supports identifiable-browser workflows once configured; a store integration alone is not proof that every browse event is arriving. Its <a class="link" href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/360020919731">predictive analytics</a> require sufficient qualifying data. Do not make predictive CLV a mandatory branch until the account and profile fields are populated and understood.</p>
<p>Yotpo documents a <a class="link" href="https://www.yotpo.com/integrations/klaviyo/">Klaviyo integration</a> for review and loyalty data. That offers a way to preserve useful content and reward context alongside a replacement sender. Confirm supported plans, credentials and field/event scope for each module. This is an integration architecture, not proof that all existing canvas logic or historical messaging events transfer automatically.</p>
<h2>Profile reconciliation before flow recreation</h2>
<p>Start with saved Yotpo exports, Shopify customer/order data and any existing Klaviyo profiles. Build a field map with source identifier, destination property type, timestamp, null behavior and conflict precedence. Include channel-specific permission evidence, exclusions, review identifiers, loyalty identifiers and subscription state. A profile merge must not promote an unsubscribed address or phone to subscribed.</p>
<p>Test one customer with a changed email, two source identifiers and different SMS permission. Compare expected identity and suppression before and after import. Quarantine ambiguous merges. Track missing event history separately from missing customer fields; importing a total spend value cannot reconstruct a sequence of placed orders. Since the old messaging service is decommissioned, recovery plans must state which saved records exist rather than promising a fresh dashboard export.</p>
<h2>Outdoor gear: 2,400 orders and $112k per month</h2>
<p>Use this proposed baseline to distinguish ultralight research from family camping bundles. Enable the required Shopify tracking and inspect a staff-owned identified profile through Viewed Product, cart, checkout and order events. Test an anonymous visit separately; do not infer identity from a product tag. Compare payload product/variant IDs with the catalog used in the message.</p>
<p>Acceptance is correct entry and exit for the two education branches, including a gift order and a tent becoming unavailable before send. Use a deterministic order/history branch where predictive fields are absent. Measure classification errors and manual fallback work; no deliverability, conversion or unsubscribe improvement is claimed.</p>
<h2>Keyboard and board-game preorders: event order matters</h2>
<p>For keyboards, retain the proposed 940 orders/$58k monthly baseline and six-week group-buy with $180–$420 kits. Test a ten-day education journey across switch-type pages, then close the preorder before its recovery timer expires. Record the event name and source timestamp used by the filter. A historical replay must not reopen the closed offer. Six hours weekly may be a labor-saving target to measure, not a reported result.</p>
<p>For board games, use 1,050 orders/$67k monthly before Essen. Map allocation, partial fulfillment and shipment states explicitly; an order alone does not mean a preorder has arrived. Choose which state starts a review request and which releases winback. Replay an allocation update after a shipment and verify it cannot reverse the later state. Count support contacts in a planned comparison if clearer status messaging is a goal; no reduction has been observed here.</p>
<h2>Tea and coffee: balances are not subscription permission</h2>
<p>Tea’s proposed 720 orders/$44k monthly fixture uses 28–35 day consumption windows. Preserve blend preference and gift-buyer status independently of permission. Submit a subscription skip after replenishment enrollment and require the next promotion to exit. Include missing preference, null and changed-value cases so a default blend cannot silently overwrite known data.</p>
<p>Coffee’s proposed 860 orders/$52k monthly fixture uses roast-date replenishment and wholesale samples. Retain Loyalty only if its balance and redemption history remain useful to the program. Test an earn event, redemption and correction before the next message; stale points should suppress the balance claim or use approved nonnumeric copy. Keep the Loyalty system authoritative and assign a connector failure owner instead of copying an unversioned balance into every template.</p>
<h2>Climbing education and review-content continuity</h2>
<p>Climbing’s proposed 1,100 orders/$89k monthly fixture covers seven-to-fourteen day harness research. Identify the fit and fall-rating pages without inferring certification or safe suitability. Keep product claims tied to manufacturer documentation. A late order must stop obsolete browse education; an unresolved safety question belongs with trained support.</p>
<p>For existing Reviews, reconcile product/variant IDs, review IDs, image reuse rights and returned orders before enabling requests or email widgets. Keeping the existing review store can preserve familiar content and reduce remapping work. It does not authorize duplicate requests: choose whether Yotpo or Klaviyo owns each request job. Test a return before dispatch and a negative review routed to support without inventing a positive outcome.</p>
<h2>Queue and credential ownership at cutover</h2>
<p>Inventory welcome, cart, browse, review, loyalty and newsletter jobs across all active apps and Shopify. For each, record sender, trigger, permission purpose, purchase exit, replay key and incident owner. Disable overlapping enrollment and remove obsolete queued work before enabling replacement enrollment. A priority list cannot cancel a second sender’s queue. A forty-five-minute timer is only a proposed race-condition test.</p>
<p>Use staff recipients for a queued cart, later purchase, opt-out and connector reconnect. Require logs showing final eligibility and cancellation, not just screenshots of an empty canvas. When a connector fails, hold dependent content or use an approved fallback; do not restart retired messaging. Restrict credentials by module, name the rotation owner and record how a failed integration is detected. Marketing, support and the integration maintainer need an explicit escalation handoff.</p>
<h2>Matched economics with goods cost held fixed</h2>
<p>Use 20,000 contacts as a proposed procurement fixture, with a separate 12,000–18,000-contact sensitivity case. Quote billable profiles, eligible email sends, SMS destinations/segments, required integrations, support and minimums on the same twelve-month calendar. Separate continuing Reviews/Loyalty costs from Klaviyo and migration labor. A historical bundle discount is not a current Yotpo messaging offer.</p>
<p>For a hypothetical $1,000 gross sale, $100 discount and $400 fixed goods cost, contribution before other costs is $500. Subtracting $20 incremental messaging leaves $480 before other variable costs. The goods cost stays $400; do not multiply discounted sales by the original margin and call that fixed-cost accounting. A planned randomized holdout should estimate incremental orders and uncertainty before that contribution is used to justify migration expense.</p>
<p>Newsletter acceptance deserves its own fixture: change the hero product after stock sells out, inspect catalog links and fallback content, and confirm exclusions for current purchasers. Compare editing, QA and exception labor before claiming savings. Capture tools or another email provider may be evaluated against a specific unmet requirement; this comparison supplies no automatic Sequenzy recommendation.</p>
<h2>Procure the sender and retain modules independently</h2>
<p>Accept Klaviyo for the demonstrated lifecycle scope when identity, permission, Shopify events, required module fields and queue exits pass and the matched quote is affordable. Hold flows relying on absent predictions or unmapped events until a defined fallback passes. Keep Yotpo Reviews/Loyalty where their content and program obligations remain valuable; replace them only through a separate migration decision. Renewing retired Email/SMS is not an alternative. Use the <a class="link" href="/guides/selection-framework">selection framework</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing-models">pricing guide</a> to record acceptance and unresolved requirements.</p>
</article>

      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-[#24170f]">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/yotpo-email-sms-vs-klaviyo">Yotpo vs Klaviyo</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/omnisend-vs-yotpo-email-sms">Omnisend vs Yotpo</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/reviews-and-ugc">Reviews and UGC</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Klaviyo vs Yotpo Email & SMS FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
